---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { apps } from '../../data/site';
const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'killmyapps')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="KillMyApps">
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">Android · Open Source · {app.version}</span>
      <h1>{app.name}</h1>
      <p>{app.tagline}</p>
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Download</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>View on GitHub</a></div>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>A focused system utility.</h2>
      <p>{app.description}</p>
      <p>KillMyApps is designed around a simple idea: when you need to stop an Android app, the action should be immediate and predictable. It avoids becoming a general app manager and keeps the interface centered on termination.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <span class="eyebrow">Capabilities</span>
      <h2>Built for power users.</h2>
      <div class="grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>One-tap kill</h3><p>Terminate selected applications quickly without navigating through system settings.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Root & Shizuku</h3><p>Use Root or Shizuku as the execution layer, depending on the device and setup.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Source-aware selection</h3><p>Work with User, Launchable, and System sources to keep selection flexible while reducing accidental operations.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Selection Mode</h3><p>Select exactly the applications you want to terminate instead of relying on a fixed list.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Auto Killer</h3><p>Automate app termination for background applications that repeatedly consume resources.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Quick access</h3><p>Trigger the utility through Quick Settings and home-screen widgets when speed matters.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>System-level approach</h2>
      <p>KillMyApps sits close to Android's process-management layer and uses privileged execution paths instead of trying to simulate termination through the regular application APIs.</p>
      <p>The project supports both Root and Shizuku, giving users different ways to provide the required system-level access while keeping the application itself lightweight.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <span class="eyebrow">Project details</span>
      <div class="meta">
        <div class="meta-row"><span>Version</span><strong>{app.version}</strong></div>
        <div class="meta-row"><span>Minimum Android</span><strong>{app.minAndroid}</strong></div>
        <div class="meta-row"><span>License</span><strong>{app.license}</strong></div>
        <div class="meta-row"><span>Execution</span><strong>Root / Shizuku</strong></div>
        <div class="meta-row"><span>Status</span><strong>{app.status}</strong></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose">
      <h2>Why it exists</h2>
      <p>Persistent background processes can consume memory, battery, and other idle resources. KillMyApps provides a direct tool for users who want explicit control over which applications are allowed to keep running.</p>
      <p>The product principle is deliberately narrow: fast execution, clear control, and no unnecessary management features.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Get KillMyApps</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>Explore the source</a></div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
